## Using the generated CSS

"Get CSS Code" lists only the variables that differ from the default theme. Put them in **your** stylesheet,
**after** the engine's CSS:

```css
/* app/assets/tailwind/application.css */
@import "tailwindcss";
@import "<gem path>/app/components/appica_ui/index.css";

/* ← paste the overrides here */
:root,
.light {
  --primary: oklch(56.26% 0.1129 283.38);
  --primary-subtle: oklch(56.26% 0.1129 283.38 / 10%);
  /* … */
}
.dark { /* … */ }
```

Don't paste them into `appica_ui/theme.css` or use them **instead** of it. `theme.css` holds every default token and the
`@theme inline` block that turns each variable into its Tailwind utilities (`--primary` → `bg-primary`,
`text-primary`, `ring-primary`…). Without it those utilities aren't generated at all, so buttons, badges and inputs
lose their colors - which is what happens when the overrides replace it.

Both blocks are plain unlayered CSS, so the later one wins: yours, because it comes after the import. Tailwind
compiles `bg-primary` to `background-color: var(--primary)`, so changing the variable repaints every component
without touching their classes.

The output is identical to appica.dev's "Get CSS Code": the generator (`theme.js`) is a port of theirs, with the same
Tailwind color scales, the same token → shade mapping and the same custom-color scale.

## How a theme is built

| Choice | Tokens | Taken from |
|--------|--------|------------|
| Base color | foreground, background, border, focus ring, shadow, selection | the neutral scale's shades (foreground 600, border 200… in light; 300, 800… in dark) |
| Primary "Base" | `--primary*` | the same neutral (900 / white) |
| Primary color | `--primary`, `-subtle`, `-soft`, `-muted`, `-strong`, `--focus-ring-primary` | the accent's 500, 500/10%, 500/20%, 400, 600 |
| Secondary color | `--secondary*`, `--focus-ring-secondary` | the accent's 300 (400 in dark), 200, 400, 500, 600 |
| Custom color | same as an accent | a 50-950 scale generated around your color |
| Radius | `--radius` | None 0 … Extra Large 1.375rem (every `rounded-*` derives from it) |
| Font | `--font-sans` | a Google Fonts family - add its `<link>` (shown in the output) to your layout |

## In your app

The block works outside Lookbook too - e.g. a settings page where users pick their theme:

```ruby
ThemeCustomizer()
```

```js
// app/javascript/application.js - every page applies the stored theme and follows changes
import { watchStoredTheme } from "appica_ui/blocks/theme_customizer/theme"
watchStoredTheme()
```

`theme.js` also exports `overridesCss(theme)` (the CSS text), `applyTheme(theme)`, `loadTheme()` / `saveTheme()` and
`customScale(color)`. The panel emits `appica-ui--blocks--theme-customizer:change` with `{ theme, css }` - save it on the server
there to make it permanent.

Mode: **Light** and **Dark** always apply; **System** follows the OS unless the page chose its own mode
(`data-theme-locked` on `<html>` - Lookbook sets it when you use its Theme display option).

